/** @page ncurses_mouseguide Mouse Support

    @tableofcontents

    @section ncursesmouseevents Events and Coordinates

    Mouse reporting is enabled by default in @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session "Session"; the
    \c mouse constructor option or @ref Qore::Ncurses::Session::setMouse() "Session::setMouse()"
    controls it. The terminal emulator must support mouse reporting.

    @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::readEvent() "Window::readEvent()" returns
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::InputEvent "InputEvent" values. When the event type is
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::InputType "InputType::Mouse", its \c mouse field contains a
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::MouseEvent "MouseEvent": zero-based screen coordinates \c x and \c y,
    and a \c button_state bitmask. Coordinates are relative to the terminal, even when input
    is read through a smaller window; subtract @ref Qore::Ncurses::Window::position() "Window::position()"
    before treating them as local drawing coordinates.

    Test button flags with bitwise AND, for example
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::BUTTON1_CLICKED "BUTTON1_CLICKED" for a left click or
    @ref Qore::Ncurses::BUTTON4_PRESSED "BUTTON4_PRESSED" for a wheel-up event.
    See @ref ncurses_constants "key and mouse constants" for the available masks.

    @section ncursesmouseexample Mouse and Keyboard Loop

    This program displays left-click coordinates and redraws after a resize. Press \c q to exit.
    The timeout keeps control in the application when no event is available.

    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires ncurses

Ncurses::Session session();
on_exit session.close();
Ncurses::Window window = session.screen();
window.clear();
window.drawTextClipped(0, 0, "Left-click anywhere; q quits");
window.refresh();
while (True) {
    *hash<Ncurses::InputEvent> event = window.readEvent(100, 25);
    if (!event) {
        continue;
    }
    if (event.type == Ncurses::InputType::Char && event.ch == "q") {
        break;
    }
    if (event.type == Ncurses::InputType::Mouse
            && (event.mouse.button_state & Ncurses::BUTTON1_CLICKED)) {
        window.clear();
        window.drawTextClipped(0, 0, sprintf("Click at row %d, column %d; q quits",
            event.mouse.y, event.mouse.x));
    } else if (event.type == Ncurses::InputType::Resize) {
        window.clear();
        window.drawTextClipped(0, 0, "Terminal resized; q quits");
    }
    window.refresh();
}
    @endcode

    Widget applications use @ref NcursesUi::EventDispatcher "EventDispatcher" for hit-testing
    and focus routing instead of implementing these rules for each widget. See
    @ref ncurses_ui_widgetsguide "UI Widget Toolkit" and
    @ref ncurses_testingguide "Automated Testing" for injecting mouse events in tests.
*/
